{
  "descriptionReviews": [
    {
      "goalId": "e4b38061-1f28-43ad-8371-a3e7c0e81856",
      "decision": "revise",
      "proposedDescriptionDe": "Die lernende Person kann erklären, weshalb sich bei einer geradlinigen Bewegung mit konstanter Beschleunigung die Geschwindigkeit in gleichen Zeitintervallen um denselben vorzeichenbehafteten Wert ändert. Sie kann beschreiben, wie sich dieser Zusammenhang in Zeitdiagrammen für Ort, Geschwindigkeit und Beschleunigung zeigt.",
      "proposedDescriptionEn": "The learner can explain why, in straight-line motion with constant acceleration, velocity changes by the same signed amount in equal time intervals. They can describe how this relationship appears in position–time, velocity–time and acceleration–time graphs.",
      "understandingEvidence": {
        "essentialUnderstandingDe": "Bei konstanter Beschleunigung gilt in jedem Zeitintervall Δv = a·Δt. Der Beschleunigungsgraph ist waagerecht, und der Geschwindigkeitsgraph ist eine Gerade mit der Steigung a. Die Steigung des Ortsgraphen gibt die momentane Geschwindigkeit an. Für a ≠ 0 ist der Ortsgraph eine Parabel, deren Krümmungsrichtung vom Vorzeichen von a abhängt.",
        "essentialUnderstandingEn": "With constant acceleration, Δv = a·Δt over every time interval. The acceleration graph is horizontal, and the velocity graph is a straight line with slope a. The slope of the position graph gives the instantaneous velocity. For nonzero acceleration, the position graph is a parabola whose concavity depends on the sign of a.",
        "observablePerformanceDe": "Aus neuen Geschwindigkeitswerten mit zugehörigen Zeiten und einem Anfangsort bestimmt die lernende Person für ein Intervall mit angenommener konstanter Beschleunigung selbstständig a in m/s². Sie zeichnet drei zueinander passende Zeitdiagramme mit Größen und Einheiten an den Achsen und erklärt an einem Teilintervall, wie die Steigung des Ortsgraphen und die Ortsänderung zu den Geschwindigkeiten passen.",
        "observablePerformanceEn": "Given fresh velocity values with their times and an initial position, the learner independently determines a in m/s² for an interval in which acceleration is assumed constant. They construct three consistent time graphs with quantities and units on the axes and use one subinterval to explain how the slope of the position graph and the displacement agree with the velocities.",
        "transferExpectationDe": "In einer neuen Aufgabe ist die Anfangsgeschwindigkeit positiv, und eine konstante negative Beschleunigung wirkt über den Stillstandsmoment hinaus. Die lernende Person sagt selbstständig den Verlauf aller drei Graphen voraus. Sie erklärt, weshalb der Ort beim Nulldurchgang der Geschwindigkeit ein Maximum erreicht und der Geschwindigkeitsbetrag danach bei gleicher Beschleunigung wieder wächst.",
        "transferExpectationEn": "In a fresh task, the initial velocity is positive and a constant negative acceleration continues beyond the instant of rest. The learner independently predicts all three graphs. They explain why position reaches a maximum as velocity crosses zero and why speed then increases again under the same acceleration."
      },
      "rationale": "The current wording identifies uniform acceleration by restating that acceleration is constant. Naming equal signed velocity changes over equal time intervals gives the learner a physical relationship to explain in all three graphs. This remains one competence across the compiled Sek I and Sek II scopes and requires no particular calculation method.",
      "evidenceProfileContract": "positive-understanding-evidence-v2",
      "evidenceProfileRecommendation": "none"
    }
  ],
  "profileReviews": [
    {
      "goalId": "e4b38061-1f28-43ad-8371-a3e7c0e81856",
      "decision": "keep",
      "reason": "Both bilingual cases are physically consistent. In the first, velocity increases by 0.40 m/s each second, giving a = +0.40 m/s² under the stated model. From 1 to 2 s the mean velocity is 0.60 m/s, in agreement with the 0.60 m displacement. The brief distinguishes what the samples establish from the assumption of constancy between them. In the reversal case, a = −0.50 m/s² gives successive positions of 0, 0.75, 1.00, 0.75 and 0 m. At 2 s velocity crosses zero and position reaches its maximum while acceleration stays negative. At 4 s the cart is back at the origin with velocity −1.0 m/s. Its subsequent motion requires further information. The two briefs cover all three required expectations and support independent demonstrations by changing both the input representation and the relative signs of velocity and acceleration. The signed-area check uses a direct prerequisite, accepts elementary reasoning and requires no calculus. Both tasks use supplied model information and require no physical experiment.",
      "requiredChanges": []
    }
  ],
  "limitations": [
    "Applicability was checked against the supplied compiled national scope. The underlying state curriculum documents and the compilation process were not independently inspected.",
    "All three rendered book pages and the listed primary image were inspected. The separate image reference `-` did not resolve to a file."
  ]
}
